@* Batch F2, 6-ARCH (user directive 2026-08-20, near-verbatim): "it should
   use the same underlying data structure as the hover menu everywhere else
   - showing sequential verses as passages (which can be expanded to read
   the whole chapter at will) rather than just verses. reuse the bits that
   we have! if you can't reuse them, then the bits need refactored for
   composability."

   THE shared, composable passage-list component: every verse LIST in the
   popover platform renders through this one component --
   CrossRefsSection (req 6), PlaceDatesSection's own est/dest supporting
   verses (req 6b), and CatechismScripturesSection ("THE SCRIPTURES").
   Sequential verses display as ONE passage entry (ref-range + contiguous
   text), never N separate verse rows (Explore/PassageBlock.cs's own
   PassageBlockBuilder, reusing Batch D's PlaceCard grouping algorithm --
   see Explore/PassageGrouping.cs). Every entry is independently
   expandable in place to read the whole chapter (MiniReaderExpand.razor,
   the SAME mechanism Batch R's verse-text section already uses -- see that
   component's own header). Truncation (Cap) counts PASSAGE ENTRIES, not
   raw verses, per requirement 6/6b verbatim; a null Cap never truncates
   (THE SCRIPTURES: no cap asked for). The down-arrow reveal/up-arrow
   snap-back interaction mirrors Batch D's own place-card-more/-collapse
   language (a plain ▾/▲ glyph, no incremental step -- "reveals the rest"
   is req 6/6b's own wording, a binary show-all rather than PlaceCard's own
   5/2-verse-at-a-time reveal, which is a DIFFERENT truncation model this
   batch doesn't touch -- see PlaceCard.razor's own comment on why it stays
   on its own mechanism).

   Batch N2 requirement 2 (folded into Batch T's own event-witnesses work,
   which needed the identical mechanism for PARALLEL ACCOUNTS -- "no more
   than 2 verses of a passage at a time... a button to expand/collapse that
   passage (and also read the whole chapter like we've already got)", the
   owner verbatim): ClampVerses caps a PASSAGE entry's own COMPACT text at N
   verses, independent of (and orthogonal to) Cap above, which counts whole
   ENTRIES -- this clamps WITHIN one entry.

   O2 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23: "we have redundant
   buttons, too. Show the rest of this passage and Read the whole
   chapter"): the per-entry `popover-passage-clamp-expand{-ENTRY-ID}/
   -collapse` TOGGLE that used to sit right here -- reveal/re-hide the rest
   of THAT entry's own clamped text, independent of and alongside
   MiniReaderExpand's own chapter-wide expand on the SAME entry -- is
   RETIRED; it was exactly the "redundant pair" the owner named (two
   different "show more" controls on one entry). ClampVerses' own STATIC
   clamp -- how many verses of a passage entry's text show before either
   arrow is ever touched -- is UNCHANGED and still computed below; only the
   separate per-entry TOGGLE that used to grow it a little further (without
   yet reaching the whole chapter) is gone. MiniReaderExpand's own control
   (now a RevealControls-driven down/double-down/up/double-up arrow pair,
   see its own O2 comment) is the SOLE remaining expansion affordance on a
   clamped entry -- revealing more of it now always means the whole,
   lazy-fetched chapter, never a partial reveal of just this entry's own
   remaining verses; a disclosed simplification (the nuance the old
   per-entry toggle offered is honestly lost) in service of "ONE expansion
   idiom... passage text, cross-refs, catechism all use the identical
   component," the controller's own words for this ruling. Implemented
   ONCE here so every consumer (xrefs, THE SCRIPTURES, place est/dest,
   PRIOR/FOLLOWING event sections, EVENT witnesses) inherits the static
   clamp for free -- "parallel implementations are a defect," the owner's
   own F2 6-ARCH decree, extended to this requirement too. A lone-verse
   entry, or a passage entry whose own verse count is already <=
   ClampVerses, is never clamped at all (conditional) -- unaffected,
   unchanged. *@
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore

@if (_blocks.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="popover-passage-list">
        @foreach (var entry in Visible)
        {
            @* The WHOLE entry -- ref label + caption + compact text -- is
               ONE explorable target, matching PlaceCard.razor's own
               .place-card-passage/.place-card-verse precedent exactly (ref
               + text together inside one clickable container), not a
               separate ref-only button beside a plain paragraph. A real,
               live-caught fix: an earlier draft put the testid on a
               ref-only <button>, which left `.toContainText(...)`
               assertions against that testid seeing only the ref, never
               the verse text sitting beside it -- see this batch's own
               report for the full story. `@onclick:stopPropagation` on the
               expand button/reader region below (MiniReaderExpand.razor)
               is what keeps "expand to read the whole chapter" and
               "explore this passage" as two independent gestures on the
               SAME entry, mirroring ONE-RULE's own "the more specific
               target always wins" rule for a passage's nested per-verse
               spans. *@
            <div class="popover-passage-entry explorable" data-testid="@entry.TestId"
                 tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="@($"Explore {entry.Block.Span}")"
                 @onclick="() => Explore(entry.Block)" @onkeydown="@(e => OnKeyDown(e, entry.Block))">
                <span class="popover-passage-ref-label">@entry.Block.Span</span>
                @if (entry.Block.Caption is { } caption)
                {
                    <span class="popover-passage-caption">@caption</span>
                }
                <MiniReaderExpand Book="@BookOf(entry.Block)" Chapter="@ChapterOf(entry.Block)" FocalFromVerse="@FocalFromOf(entry.Block)" FocalToVerse="@FocalToOf(entry.Block)"
                                   TestIdSuffix="@($"-{entry.TestId}")" TruncatedBy="@entry.Block.TruncatedBy" OnExplore="OnExplore">
                    <CompactContent>
                        @* M-D1 requirement 3 (owner live report #4, verbatim:
                           "it also is completely redundant to just show the
                           verses associated with a container in the
                           container's hover box. we should just see the
                           passage span."): SpanOnly renders NOTHING here --
                           the ref label above (@entry.Block.Span) is already
                           the compact SPAN reference; the ONLY affordance
                           left is MiniReaderExpand's own expand control
                           below, which reuses the identical inline-expand
                           mechanism every other passage entry already has
                           (reuse, not a parallel implementation -- the
                           requirement's own explicit instruction). No
                           verse-list echo, no clamp (nothing to clamp when
                           nothing renders by default). *@
                        @if (!SpanOnly)
                        {
                            // Batch N2 requirement 2, STATIC half (the toggle
                            // that used to grow this further -- O2's own
                            // "redundant pair" -- is retired; see this file's
                            // own header comment): clamp THIS entry's own
                            // compact text to ClampVerses verses, independent
                            // of MiniReaderExpand's own (unaffected) chapter
                            // expand below. Only ever narrows a PASSAGE entry
                            // (IsPassage, >=2 verses) whose own verse count
                            // exceeds the clamp -- a lone verse, or a passage
                            // already at or under it, is never clamped.
                            var clamps = entry.Block.IsPassage && ClampVerses is int clamp && entry.Block.Verses.Count > clamp;
                            var shown = clamps ? entry.Block.Verses.Take(ClampVerses!.Value) : entry.Block.Verses;
                            @* M-D4 fix round 1 (R-M1, review Important-1 --
                               decision 3, "name links everywhere... render
                               wherever verse text renders"): every entry's
                               own compact preview text now scans for in-text
                               place/person mentions too, through the SAME
                               MentionText.razor component VerseLine.razor/
                               VerseTextSection.razor already use -- this was
                               the one remaining bare, unscanned rendering of
                               verse text client-wide (grep-confirmed by
                               review). TestIdSuffix mirrors MiniReaderExpand's
                               own established multi-instance disambiguation
                               one line below it (this entry's own FULL
                               testid), so two different entries citing the
                               SAME verse number never collide. OnExplore is
                               the SAME callback this entry's own outer click
                               already uses -- a mention click stops
                               propagation (MentionText's own contract) so it
                               opens THAT entity, never ALSO this whole
                               entry's own Explore(entry.Block). *@
                            <p class="popover-passage-text">
                                @if (entry.Block.IsPassage)
                                {
                                    @foreach (var v in shown)
                                    {
                                        <sup class="popover-passage-verse-num">@CanonRef.ParseVerse(v.Vref).Verse</sup><MentionText Text="@v.Text" Places="@(v.Places ?? Array.Empty<PlaceRefDto>())" Persons="@(v.Persons ?? Array.Empty<PersonRefDto>())"
                                                     Verse="@CanonRef.ParseVerse(v.Vref).Verse" ClassPrefix="popover-passage-mention" TestIdSuffix="@($"-{entry.TestId}")" OnExplore="OnExplore" /><text> </text>
                                    }
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    var lone = entry.Block.Verses[0];
                                    <MentionText Text="@lone.Text" Places="@(lone.Places ?? Array.Empty<PlaceRefDto>())" Persons="@(lone.Persons ?? Array.Empty<PersonRefDto>())"
                                                 Verse="@CanonRef.ParseVerse(lone.Vref).Verse" ClassPrefix="popover-passage-mention" TestIdSuffix="@($"-{entry.TestId}")" OnExplore="OnExplore" />
                                }
                            </p>
                        }
                    </CompactContent>
                </MiniReaderExpand>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
    @* O3 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23, xrefs named
       specifically, ruled universal for the shared reveal component --
       "that button should be below the list of xrefs, not above"): moved
       from BEFORE the list to AFTER it. M-D3/U2's own original placement
       here was a deliberate, disclosed WORKAROUND for a real, live-caught
       bug -- a genuine rapid native DOUBLE-CLICK's second sub-click landing
       on a newly-revealed entry instead of the (now-shifted) control, once
       the list grew beneath a TRAILING control (RevealControls.razor's own
       header comment has the full story) -- not a preference of its own.
       R-D3 already retired the hazard that workaround existed for: "double"
       is now a SECOND, STATIONARY arrow button beside the single one, never
       a double-click gesture on one moving target, so there is no second
       sub-click that could ever land on shifted content. Safe to honor the
       owner's own placement now that the reason to defy it is gone. *@
    @if (Cap is int cap && _blocks.Count > cap)
    {
        <RevealControls Total="@_blocks.Count" Default="@cap" Shown="@ShownCount(cap)" ShownChanged="@(n => _shown = n)"
                         MoreTestId="@MoreTestId" CollapseTestId="@CollapseTestId" RevealNoun="@RevealNoun" />
    }
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<PassageSourceUnit> Units { get; set; } = Array.Empty<PassageSourceUnit>();
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string RefTestIdPrefix { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public int? Cap { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string MoreTestId { get; set; } = "passage-list-more";
    [Parameter] public string CollapseTestId { get; set; } = "passage-list-collapse";
    [Parameter] public string RevealNoun { get; set; } = "entries";
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<IExplorable> OnExplore { get; set; }

    // Batch N2 requirement 2: when set, caps a PASSAGE entry's own compact
    // text at this many verses -- see this file's own header comment.
    [Parameter] public int? ClampVerses { get; set; }

    // M-D1 requirement 3 (span-not-echo, owner live report #4): when true,
    // every entry renders its own ref-label span ONLY -- no compact text --
    // leaving MiniReaderExpand's own control (O2: a RevealControls-driven
    // arrow pair, "Read the whole chapter" now living in its title/
    // aria-label rather than visible button text) as the sole way to see
    // the passage's own words. Default false: every OTHER consumer
    // (cross-references, THE SCRIPTURES, place est/dest, PRIOR/FOLLOWING,
    // multi-witness PARALLEL ACCOUNTS) is UNCHANGED by this batch -- see
    // this file's own header comment block for the full rationale.
    [Parameter] public bool SpanOnly { get; set; }

    // A real, live-caught regression (found by the full pre-existing suite,
    // not guessed): CrossRefsSection's OWN pre-Batch-F2 click contract --
    // every xref-item, REGARDLESS of whether its target spans more than one
    // verse (~25% of real cross-ref targets do, e.g. "REV.8.3-5" -- common,
    // not an edge case), always pushed a VerseNode at the target's own
    // FIRST verse (CanonRef.FirstVerseOf), never a PassageNode -- reader.spec.ts's
    // own READ-3 property test pins exactly this ("popover-title" ==
    // the target's bare head ref after a click, for ANY sampled xref). The
    // default below (PassageNode for a multi-verse block) is right for THE
    // SCRIPTURES/place est-dest, where a question's or a claim's own cited
    // passage genuinely IS the explorable unit -- but wrong for a single
    // cross-reference, whose own identity is "where it points", not "the
    // span its preview text happens to cover". CrossRefsSection sets this
    // true to restore its own pre-existing, still-tested contract; every
    // other consumer leaves it at the default.
    [Parameter] public bool ExploreAsVerse { get; set; }

    // A block's own Span is not always unique WITHIN one list -- the same
    // verse can legitimately be cited more than once from different
    // SOURCES (e.g. a catechism item's own item-level embedded citation
    // AND one or more of its own questions all naming the same verse --
    // MAT.28.19 under baptism-1 is a real example, cited bare AND by four
    // separate repo-derived questions). Real, caught live (not guessed):
    // every block gets a testid built from `{RefTestIdPrefix}-{Span}`, and
    // the SECOND and later block sharing the same Span gets a numbered
    // suffix (`--2`, `--3`, ...) so it never collides with an earlier one
    // -- same disambiguation shape CatechismSeamSection already applies to
    // repeated `catechism-item-{ID}` rows, applied here generically so
    // EVERY passage-list consumer (xrefs, THE SCRIPTURES, place est/dest)
    // is protected, not just the one case that happened to be found first.
    private sealed record VisibleEntry(PassageBlockData Block, string TestId);

    private List<PassageBlockData> _blocks = new();

    // M-D3/U2: how many blocks are currently revealed -- null until first
    // touched, at which point it starts at Cap (the default view), same
    // "starts at the collapsed default, never auto-resets on an unrelated
    // re-render" risk profile the retired `_showAll` bool always had (this
    // component is freshly instantiated per popover-section resolve, not
    // reused across genuinely different Units -- see RevealControls.razor's
    // own doc comment for the fuller reasoning). RevealControls owns the
    // actual +2/-2/all/default arithmetic; this field is purely the
    // CURRENT VALUE it proposes changes to via ShownChanged.
    private int? _shown;

    private int ShownCount(int cap) => _shown ??= cap;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        _blocks = PassageBlockBuilder.Build(Units);
    }

    private IEnumerable<VisibleEntry> Visible
    {
        get
        {
            var shown = Cap is int cap ? _blocks.Take(ShownCount(cap)) : _blocks;
            var seen = new Dictionary<string, int>();
            foreach (var block in shown)
            {
                var occurrence = seen[block.Span] = seen.GetValueOrDefault(block.Span) + 1;
                var testId = occurrence == 1 ? $"{RefTestIdPrefix}-{block.Span}" : $"{RefTestIdPrefix}-{block.Span}--{occurrence}";
                yield return new VisibleEntry(block, testId);
            }
        }
    }

    private static string BookOf(PassageBlockData b) => CanonRef.ParseVerse(b.FirstVref).Book;
    private static int ChapterOf(PassageBlockData b) => CanonRef.ParseVerse(b.FirstVref).Chapter;
    private static int FocalFromOf(PassageBlockData b) => CanonRef.ParseVerse(b.FirstVref).Verse;
    private static int FocalToOf(PassageBlockData b) => CanonRef.ParseVerse(b.LastVref).Verse;

    private Task Explore(PassageBlockData block)
    {
        IExplorable node = block.IsPassage && !ExploreAsVerse
            ? new PassageNode(block.Span, string.Join(" ", block.Verses.Select(v => v.Text)))
            : new VerseNode(block.FirstVref);
        return OnExplore.InvokeAsync(node);
    }

    // Keyboard equivalent (design-direction.md: "Enter opens") -- same
    // Enter-only scope every other explorable element in this app uses
    // (Reader.razor's OnVerseLineKeyDown, PlaceCard's own OnVerseKeyDown/
    // OnPassageKeyDown).
    private Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e, PassageBlockData block) => e.Key == "Enter" ? Explore(block) : Task.CompletedTask;
}
